A column schema.sql no longer makes would lose its values: refused, and
nothing changes. The error gives the line that drops it on purpose, and
the one that would fill a renamed column from it.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'soup', 'hot');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;

== error
column `dish.note` is in the database, not in schema.sql: its values would go. Keep it, or drop it on purpose: `-- @drop 1 column dish note` in schema.sql (with `-- @release 1`). Renamed? Drop it, and fill the new one from it: `-- @from 1 column dish <new> = "note"`.
